WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.000
Light and air

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:09.000
Photoperiod at Earth gravity

00:00:07.667 --> 00:00:14.667
In darkness the seedling stretches its hypocotyl and keeps its hook closed. Light stops the stretching and opens the hook: de-etiolation.

00:00:31.000 --> 00:00:39.000
Hypocotyl at 120 h: 7.5 mm in the dark, 2.3 mm with a day-night cycle and 2.2 mm in continuous light.

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:45.000
Habitat atmospheres and oxygen

00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:51.000
Exploration habitats would lower cabin pressure and raise the oxygen fraction. Colours show oxygen inside the seed.

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:08.000
Endosperm rupture: 32.0 h at sea level, 33.5 h in the exploration atmosphere (19.2 kPa of oxygen).

00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:19.000
With ordinary air at that pressure (11.9 kPa of oxygen) it slips to 49.9 h, and at 5.1 kPa to 63.7 h. What matters is the oxygen partial pressure.

00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:25.000
Airflow past the seed, in microgravity

00:01:24.250 --> 00:01:31.250
In microgravity warm air does not rise, so only fans move air past the seed. Here the air speed is the only difference.

00:01:39.250 --> 00:01:48.250
The fan raises mass transfer through the air (Sherwood number 2.0 to 3.4), yet at 30 h the radicle tip sits at 6.0 versus 6.1 kPa of oxygen, and the core of the axis at 0.0 and 0.0 kPa.

00:01:48.625 --> 00:01:54.625
The seed coat and the respiring tissue limit the oxygen supply, not the air around the seed.
